.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 21, 2026
.Dt HYPRBSD-NETWORK 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm hyprbsd-network
.Nd Wi-Fi networks and network card settings
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Cm list
.Op Fl -scan
.Nm
.Cm join
.Op Fl -hidden
.Ar name
.Nm
.Cm forget
.Ar name
.Nm
.Cm status
.Op Fl -follow
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
lists, joins and forgets Wi-Fi networks through
.Xr wpa_supplicant 8 Ns 's
control socket, as a member of the
.Ql wheel
group, and says how the machine is connected.
Its root helper,
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/libexec/hyprbsd-network-helper ,
sets Wi-Fi up for that; see
.Sx FILES .
Network names go to wpa_supplicant as bytes, in hex, and never through a shell.
A password is hashed before wpa_supplicant sees it, so its file holds the key, not the
password.
.Pp
Without a command,
.Nm
opens its window, or asks the one already open to come forward.
The window lists the networks in range, strongest first, after the one joined, with their
signal and a padlock on those that aren't open, then the saved networks out of range.
A network that can't be joined, such as a WPA3-only one, says why.
Selecting a network opens it:
a new network with a password asks for it, then Connect joins it;
a saved network has Connect, Forget, and Connect automatically, which says whether
wpa_supplicant joins it by itself.
A wrong password, or a network out of reach, is said in words, and nothing is saved until the
network is joined.
Other network\&... joins a network that hides its name.
When it opens, and whenever wpa_supplicant starts again, the window scans unless a network is
joined; while one is, the list is the last scan's, and says how long ago that was.
Rescan scans at any time; while a network is joined that can drop the connection for a moment.
The joined network has Disconnect, after which the window scans; wpa_supplicant then joins no
network by itself until one is connected again.
The switch beside it turns Wi-Fi off, and it stays off after a restart, or on again.
Without Wi-Fi set up, the window offers Set up, which asks for an administrator's password;
without a Wi-Fi adapter, it says so.
.Pp
The Interfaces tab, at the top beside Wi-Fi, lists the network cards: wired, Wi-Fi and USB ones
(a phone sharing its connection is a USB card), whether each is connected and its address, a
Wi-Fi adapter not set up yet, a PCI network card FreeBSD has no driver for, by its make, and a
lagg with its ports.
A card's switch turns it off, and it stays off after a restart, or on; a Wi-Fi card's is the
Wi-Fi switch.
Selecting a card opens its settings: Automatic (DHCP), or Manual with its address, prefix length
(or netmask), gateway and up to three DNS servers.
Apply checks them, then, after an administrator's password, puts them in
.Xr rc.conf 5
and in effect.
The computer has one gateway: typed on a card, it moves from the card that had it, and the
settings say so before Apply.
New lagg, below the cards when two or more can be its ports, joins cards as one
.Xr lagg 4 :
its page has the mode (failover, LACP, load balance or round robin), the cards to be its
ports, ticked in order (failover uses the first while it has a link), and the settings; Create
makes it, after an administrator's password.
Nothing on it is picked for you.
With Wi-Fi among the ports, the wired ones take the Wi-Fi card's MAC address, and the page says
so.
Selecting a lagg opens the same page, with Apply, and Remove, which puts its ports back on
DHCP.
A lagg has no switch.
A card that's part of a bridge or lagg says so and has no settings; one set up in rc.conf in
ways the settings don't cover shows its rc.conf line instead, a lagg too.
.Pp
The keyboard works throughout: Tab moves between networks, cards and buttons, Enter or Space
selects, and Escape closes the open network or card settings, then the window; while a network
is being joined, forgotten or saved, or settings applied, Escape does nothing.
.Pp
The commands:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm list Op Fl -scan
The networks in range, strongest first, with their security, and which are saved or joined.
It scans first unless a network is joined, or with
.Fl -scan .
.It Cm join Oo Fl -hidden Oc Ar name
Joins the network and saves it.
The password is the first line of standard input when that isn't a terminal (passwords are
typed in the window); a saved network is joined without one.
.Fl -hidden
joins a network that hides its name.
.It Cm forget Ar name
Forgets every saved network with that name.
.It Cm status Op Fl -follow
How the machine is connected: through Wi-Fi, which network and how strong, or wired.
With
.Fl -follow
it prints a line of JSON for
.Xr waybar 5 Ns 's
custom modules, and another each time the connection changes.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev HYPRBSD_NETWORK_CTRL_DIR
wpa_supplicant's control socket directory, instead of
.Pa /var/run/wpa_supplicant .
.It Ev HYPRBSD_NETWORK_TRACE
Set, the window writes on standard error, with the time, what wpa_supplicant tells it and what
it shows, for bug reports.
.It Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where
.Nm
makes its own sockets, and where the open window listens for a second start.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/libexec/hyprbsd-network-helper Oo Fl r Ar root Oc Cm wifi-setup Oo Fl a Oc Op Ar adapter
Run as root, it gives the adapter (by default the first FreeBSD has attached) its wlan
interface in
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
with wpa_supplicant and DHCP, a control socket for
.Ql wheel ,
.Ql update_config=1 ,
so the networks joined are saved,
and
.Ql bss_expiration_age=3600 ,
so the networks a scan found stay listed for an hour; and
.Ql wpa_supplicant_umask=077 ,
so the file stays private.
Other networks and settings in the file are kept.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/libexec/hyprbsd-network-helper Oo Fl r Ar root Oc Cm wifi Cm on | off | toggle
Run as root,
.Cm off
adds
.Ql NOAUTO
to every wlan in
.Xr rc.conf 5
that uses wpa_supplicant, so it stays down after a restart, and stops it, taking its DNS
servers out of
.Xr resolvconf 8
and handing the default route on as
.Cm card-gone
does;
.Cm on
takes
.Ql NOAUTO
out and starts it;
.Cm toggle
turns Wi-Fi off while one of them is on, and on when all are off.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/libexec/hyprbsd-network-helper Oo Fl r Ar root Oc Cm iface Ar interface Cm dhcp
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/libexec/hyprbsd-network-helper Oo Fl r Ar root Oc Cm iface Ar interface Cm manual Ar address Ns / Ns Ar len Oo Ar gateway | Cm - Oc Op Ar dns ...
Run as root, sets the card's
.Va ifconfig_ Ns Ar interface
in
.Xr rc.conf 5
to DHCP or the address, keeping
.Ql WPA ,
.Ql NOAUTO ,
.Ql up ,
.Ql mtu
and
.Ql accept_rtadv ;
a line with any other setting is left as it is.
A gateway becomes
.Va defaultrouter ,
taken from the card that had it; no gateway takes away this card's.
DNS servers go to
.Xr resolvconf 8
from
.Pa /etc/start_if. Ns Ar interface
and
.Pa /etc/stop_if. Ns Ar interface ,
which
.Pa /etc/rc.d/netif
runs as it starts and stops the card.
It checks every value first.
On the running system it stops the card with its old settings, their DNS servers going with
them, and starts it with the new.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/libexec/hyprbsd-network-helper Oo Fl r Ar root Oc Cm iface-switch Ar interface Cm on | off
Run as root,
.Cm off
adds
.Ql NOAUTO
to the card's line, so it stays down after a restart, and stops it as
.Cm wifi off
stops a wlan;
.Cm on
takes
.Ql NOAUTO
out and starts it.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/libexec/hyprbsd-network-helper Oo Fl r Ar root Oc Cm lagg Ar laggN mode port,port,... Cm dhcp | manual ...
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/libexec/hyprbsd-network-helper Oo Fl r Ar root Oc Cm lagg Ar laggN Cm remove
Run as root, makes or changes the lagg
.Ar laggN
in mode
.Cm failover ,
.Cm lacp ,
.Cm loadbalance
or
.Cm roundrobin
with the ports in that order, addressed as
.Cm iface
addresses a card: it goes in
.Va cloned_interfaces ,
its line is
.Ql up laggproto Ar mode Ql laggport Ar port ...
with the address and the
.Ql mtu
and
.Ql accept_rtadv
it had, each port's line
.Ql up ,
a wlan's
.Ql WPA ,
and with a wlan among the ports, a wired port's
.Ql ether
with the wlan's MAC address.
A port it no longer has goes back to DHCP.
.Cm remove
takes the lagg out of
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
with its gateway and DNS hooks, and puts its ports back on DHCP.
It checks everything first, and leaves a lagg or port set up by hand as it is.
On the running system it stops the lagg, restarts the ports and starts the lagg;
.Cm remove
destroys the lagg, and a wired port gets its own MAC address back.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/libexec/hyprbsd-network-helper Cm card-gone Ar interface
Run as root by
.Xr devd 8
when a card's link goes down or the card goes away, and by
.Pa /etc/dhclient-exit-hooks
when its lease ends.
A card that is down or without a link keeps its addresses, but one on the network of
another card with a link becomes a /32 (a Manual card's, if it is the one in
.Xr rc.conf 5 )
until dhclient or
.Cm card-back
sets it again.
If the default route went through the card, or there is none, the route and the card's
DNS servers from DHCP go, and the route goes to the first other card that is up with a
link and takes it: a DHCP card asks for its lease again, and a Manual card on the network of
.Va defaultrouter
gets that.
If no card takes it, a Manual card keeps the route.
A card without a link loses its Manual DNS servers unless the route still goes through it.
Then, as after
.Cm iface
and switching off, the card the route goes through gets its DNS servers back into
.Xr resolvconf 8
if they are not there: a DHCP card asks for its lease again, and a Manual card runs its
.Pa /etc/start_if. Ns Ar interface
again.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/libexec/hyprbsd-network-helper Cm card-back Ar interface
Run as root by
.Xr devd 8
when a card's link comes back; it does nothing if the card is down or without a link by the
time it runs.
A Manual card's address from
.Xr rc.conf 5
at /32 gets its prefix length back; with no default route, the card gets
.Va defaultrouter
if that is on its network; its DNS servers go back in from the
.Pa /etc/start_if. Ns Ar interface
that
.Cm iface
wrote.
Then, as after
.Cm card-gone ,
the card the route goes through gets its DNS servers.
A DHCP card is left to dhclient.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/share/polkit-1/actions/org.hyprbsd.network.policy
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/share/polkit-1/rules.d/50-hyprbsd-network.rules
Who may run the helper through
.Xr pkexec 1 :
members of
.Ql wheel
at the computer,
.Cm wifi
and
.Cm iface-switch
without a password,
.Cm wifi-setup ,
.Cm iface
and
.Cm lagg
after an administrator's.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/devd/hyprbsd-network.conf
Sets a Wi-Fi adapter up the same way when it attaches, unless another attached adapter
has Wi-Fi or
.Xr rc.conf 5
has
.Ql hyprbsd_network_autosetup="NO" .
The Wi-Fi line of an adapter that is no longer attached moves to the one that attaches,
with its wlan and settings.
.It Pa /etc/wpa_supplicant.conf
The saved networks.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Usage errors exit 2.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr wpa_supplicant.conf 5 ,
.Xr ifconfig 8 ,
.Xr resolvconf 8 ,
.Xr wpa_supplicant 8
